     "result_text_tail": " nearly over, so check the dates.\n- **Sizing basis:** levers are annual run-rates at FY2025 sales levels, assuming nothing else gets worse. Interactions between levers are ignored, worth about \u00b1$1M.\n- **Lever targets I set:** promotional share back to 26% (the FY2024 level), vendor funding from 46% to 50%, a 3% net cut in labor hours, and private label at 20.5%.\n- **One-time costs:** the pack gives only the $7.5M exit cost for the three longer-lease stores. I assumed a $10M budget for everything else, or $17.5M with those three stores, within the $25M cap.\n- **Store split:** the pack gives only totals for the nine weakest stores, so I split the benefit pro rata between the six and the three.\n- **Vendor funding:** I read the 14-point promotional margin gap as already net of vendor funding. That gives about $1M a year per point of funding recovered.\n- **Lost delivery sales:** I treated all sales lost from the fee change as sales Harlow would not otherwise make, which is the cautious case.\n- **The other three weak stores:** they stay out of the base plan. They get a 12-month turnaround attempt, then a closure vote in FY2027.\n\n**Risks the deck states openly:**\n- Using only the high-confidence levers, margin reaches only about 2.0%, so the promotion, vendor and shrink levers are essential.\n- The 2027 minimum-wage rise could cost up to about $6.7M a year. The deck shows about $7.6M of backup levers to cover it.\n\nFiles are in `./deliverable/`:\n- Harlow_Margin_Plan_Board_Feb2027.pptx"
    }
